.TH CHECK-CERT 1 "March 2026" "2.0.0" "Certificate Checker Utility"
.SH NAME
check-cert \- Validate SSL certificate compatibility: cert, key, intermediate, and chain.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B check-cert
[\fB\-c\fR cert_file] [\fB\-k\fR key_file] [\fB\-i\fR intermediate_file] [\fB\-b\fR chain_file]
[\fB\-\-host\fR host:port] [\fB\-w\fR days] [\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-j\fR] [\fB\-h\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBcheck-cert\fR is a zero-dependency Bash CLI tool to verify that an SSL certificate
matches its private key, check expiry, detect self-signed certificates, validate
intermediate CA chain, verify full chain bundles, and optionally fetch certificates
directly from remote hosts.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c, --cert
Path to the certificate file to validate (.pem / .crt). Required unless --host is used.
.TP
.B \-k, --key
Path to the private key file. Required.
.TP
.B \-i, --intermediate
Path to the intermediate certificate file (optional).
.TP
.B \-b, --chain
Path to a full chain bundle file for verification (optional).
.TP
.B \-\-host
Remote host and port to fetch the certificate from (e.g. example.com:443).
.TP
.B \-w, --warn-days
Number of days before expiry to emit a warning. Default: 30.
.TP
.B \-v, --verbose
Enable verbose mode to display detailed OpenSSL output including key hashes, SANs, and issuer info.
.TP
.B \-j, --json
Output results in JSON format. Useful for CI/CD pipelines.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, --help
Show help and usage information.

.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
All checks passed.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error or missing/invalid arguments.
.TP
.B 2
One or more checks failed.

.SH EXAMPLES
check-cert -c cert.pem -k key.pem

check-cert -c cert.pem -k key.pem -i intermediate.pem -v

check-cert -c cert.pem -k key.pem -b fullchain.pem --warn-days 60

check-cert --host example.com:443 -k key.pem

check-cert -c cert.pem -k key.pem -j > result.json

